after:
also exposes the value as it but returns the original context object. This differs from let even when their bodies look alike.

In this separate worked call, also receives "sun" as its context value and exposes it as it. The lambda computes a length, but the whole also expression returns the original String; unlike let, it does not return that length.
```kotlin
fun main() {
    val original = "sun"
    val result = original.also { it.length }
    println(result)
}
```
Expected output: sun.

import java.io.Closeable makes that JVM interface available by its short name Closeable. It defines close(). throw IllegalArgumentException() creates and throws an exception, transferring control rather than returning normally. The lambda passed to use runs before close; after runs only after use returns normally. A block failure is propagated after cleanup, so ordinary following code is skipped unless a suitable handler recovers.

This complete failing-path companion constructs IllegalArgumentException with a diagnostic message and throws it inside use. The exception is not a normal return value: use closes the resource before the matching handler prints caught.
```kotlin
import java.io.Closeable
class Trace : Closeable {
    override fun close() { println("close") }
}
fun main() {
    try {
        Trace().use {
            println("body")
            throw IllegalArgumentException("stop")
        }
    } catch (error: IllegalArgumentException) {
        println("caught")
    }
}
```
Expected output: body; close; caught.

sortedWith returns a sorted list without changing the source. reversed on the complete comparator reverses both length ordering and the tie-break ordering, not merely one selected key.

order.reversed() takes this comparator as receiver and returns a comparator with the entire ordering reversed. Passing it to sortedWith changes both the length ordering and equal-length lexical order without modifying the input list.
```kotlin
fun main() {
    val order = compareBy<String> { it.length }.thenBy { it }
    val words = listOf("cat", "ant", "ox")
    println(words.sortedWith(order.reversed()))
}
```
Expected output: [cat, ant, ox].

