sort orders array values in place and replaces keys with consecutive integers. asort orders values while keeping each key attached. Both mutate the supplied array.

The explicit sort call below receives the mutable array and numeric-comparison flag. It changes that array in place and reindexes its entries to 0 and 1; the returned success Boolean is not the sorted array.
```php
<?php
$scores = ["first" => 30, "second" => 10];
sort($scores, SORT_NUMERIC);
echo implode(",", array_keys($scores)), "\n";
echo implode(",", array_values($scores)), "\n";
```
Expected output: 0,1; 10,30.

Optional local transfer task: Compare asort with ksort, which orders keys. You may trace it without a PHP interpreter; reading completion is not proof of a local run.

ksort orders the keys in place while keeping each value attached. Here SORT_STRING compares the supplied String keys lexically; the call returns success rather than a replacement array. This differs from sorting values with sort or asort.
```php
<?php
$scores = ["z" => 10, "a" => 30];
ksort($scores, SORT_STRING);
echo implode(",", array_keys($scores)), "\n";
echo implode(",", array_values($scores)), "\n";
```
Expected output: a,z; 30,10.

The session extension persists server-side data associated with an identifier; the cookie normally carries that identifier, not the entire session array. The $_SESSION superglobal array holds the active session data; unset($_SESSION["minutes"]) removes that key. session_start acquires state, and session_write_close saves it and releases the lock. Avoid retaining a session lock while unrelated slow work runs.

This isolated array-only companion shows unset removing the specified key without ending a session or destroying other data. Its input is the selected entry, and the null-coalescing read then uses the missing-key fallback. The session fixture separately verifies saving and reopening that deletion.
```php
<?php
$_SESSION = ["minutes" => 15];
unset($_SESSION["minutes"]);
echo $_SESSION["minutes"] ?? "missing", "\n";
```
Expected output: missing.
